{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed581286445/12/codegen_prompts/6.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed581286445/12`\n- 题号：`6`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed581286445/12/6.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"6\">\n<description>\n提取每个带框对象的内部图案，根据框架颜色在颜色环上的相邻关系确定拼接顺序，再根据框架缺失边的方位确定拼接方向。\n\nInput structure：\n- 背景为黑色（black）。\n- 网格中散布若干互不相连的带框矩形对象。每个对象由一个单色一像素厚的矩形框架和框架内部的多色图案组成。\n- 每个框架的四条边中恰好有一条边完全缺失（即该侧无边框像素），形成一个\"开口\"矩形（类似U形或C形）。\n- 网格中还有一个独立的\"颜色环\"图例：由若干不同颜色的像素首尾相连排成一个封闭环形（环的每个像素颜色不同，且这些颜色恰好对应所有框架的颜色）。\n\nRule：\n- 第一步：读取颜色环图例，确定框架颜色的环形顺序（顺时针方向读取）。\n- 第二步：按颜色环的顺序排列所有带框对象。相邻两个对象（在颜色环上相邻的框架颜色）需要拼接在一起。\n- 第三步：确定拼接方向——每个对象的开口方向（缺失边的方位：上/下/左/右）指示该对象需要与下一个对象拼接的方向。将下一个对象的内部图案（不含框架）放置在当前对象开口方向的外侧，紧贴开口边缘。\n- 第四步：输出为所有内部图案按上述规则依次拼接后的组合图形。第一个对象保留其完整的三面框架，后续对象只保留内部图案。框架颜色环图例不出现在输出中。\n\nKey insight：\n颜色环编码了拼接顺序——需要从环形图例中归纳出框架颜色之间的前后关系；而每个框架的开口方向编码了拼接的空间方位——两层信息分别来自全局图例和局部框架形态，必须同时正确解读才能完成拼合。\n</description>\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：eval/88e364bc, eval/dbff022c, eval/2d0172a1, eval/7b3084d4, train/ac6f9922, train/465b7d93, train/6cbe9eb8, eval/581f7754\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"88e364bc\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.4236\nmechanism=0.4936\nlexical=0.0112\ncode_feature=0.7143\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, code:rotation, object:closed_region, object:frame, object:grid_edge, object:rectangle, object:template, operation:sort_order, relation:adjacent, relation:direction, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/88e364bc.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/88e364bc.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSlide yellow pixels inside walled regions in the direction dictated by border-legend arrows.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; yellow pixels sit inside enclosed regions bounded by colored walls.\n- Border legends: small rectangles on the grid edge whose fill color C matches a region's border color. Each legend contains a center dot and an indicator dot encoding a direction.\n\nRule:\n- For each enclosed region, identify its border color C and look up the legend with fill color C.\n- The direction from the legend's center dot to its indicator dot gives the slide direction.\n- All yellow pixels in that region slide in that direction, stacking against the far wall.\n\nKey insight: Legend fill color links to region border color; the center→indicator offset encodes the movement vector.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Yellow pixels inside colored-border regions move in the direction indicated by the red pixel in the legend with matching background color, stopping at region boundary.\",\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"yellow pixels inside colored-border regions s\",\n  \"dst\": null,\n  \"match_cond\": \"\",\n  \"path\": \"move(s, direction=legend.red_pixel_offset) where legend.background_color == s.containing_region.border_color until s.touches(region_boundary)\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"\",\n  \"transform\": \"\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": \"legend on grid edge with background color C and red pixel indicates: yellow pixels in regions with border color C move in direction from blue center to red pixel\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"\",\n  \"concepts\": [\n    \"legend/control-pixels\",\n    \"region-membership\",\n    \"directional-movement\",\n    \"boundary-collision\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dbff022c\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.4199\nmechanism=0.4967\nlexical=0.0109\ncode_feature=0.6667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, object:closed_region, object:frame, object:grid_edge, object:sprite, object:template, operation:sort_order, relation:adjacent, relation:direction, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/dbff022c.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/dbff022c.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill hollow sprites' interiors using a color-palette legend on the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Several hollow sprites with distinct border colors.\n- A small 2×N or N×2 palette block sits on one grid edge; one row/column lists border colors, the other lists corresponding fill colors.\n\nRule:\n- Locate the palette legend on a grid edge and read it as a border→fill color mapping.\n- Find enclosed bg regions (holes) inside sprites that don't touch the grid boundary.\n- For each hole, identify the most common adjacent border color, look it up in the palette, and fill the hole with the mapped fill color.\n\nKey insight: The palette's edge position and orientation determine reading direction; border colors in the palette uniquely key into fill colors.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Use a color palette legend on the grid edge to fill sprites' interiors based on their border color mapping.\",\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"hollow sprites with colored borders s\",\n  \"dst\": \"interior of same sprite d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"d.fill_color == palette_lookup(s.border_color)\",\n  \"path\": \"\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"\",\n  \"transform\": \"fill d with palette[s.border_color]\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": \"palette is 2xN or Nx2 block on grid edge; read direction based on edge position: top edge reads top-to-bottom, bottom edge reads bottom-to-top, left edge reads left-to-right; first row/column is border color, second is fill color\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"Step 1: locate 2xN or Nx2 palette legend on grid edge; Step 2: build color mapping (border -> fill) based on reading direction; Step 3: for each hollow sprite, lookup border color in palette and fill interior with corresponding fill color\",\n  \"concepts\": [\n    \"color-mapping\",\n    \"lookup-table\",\n    \"flood-fill\",\n    \"border-detection\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"The challenge is correctly reading the palette legend based on its edge position and orientation\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2d0172a1\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3435\nmechanism=0.4263\nlexical=0.0111\ncode_feature=0.4286\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, object:closed_region, object:frame, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:crop_resize, operation:sort_order, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/2d0172a1.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/2d0172a1.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nConvert a rough nested line drawing into a clean canonical frame-and-dot diagram.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one background color and one foreground color.\n- Foreground 4-connected objects are rough closed frames or small filled blobs.\n- Frames may contain other frames/blobs; blobs outside a frame remain outside it.\n\nRule:\n- Detect the containment tree of foreground objects using holes of each frame object.\n- Replace every blob with one foreground pixel.\n- Replace every frame with a rectangular border padded two cells around its direct children.\n- Place siblings by their original row/column overlap and order, with one empty cell between canonical objects.\n\nKey insight: The exact wobbly geometry is irrelevant; only enclosure plus relative sibling positions define the output.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"symmetry/pattern\",\n  \"src\": \"rough two-color nested foreground drawing: closed frame objects and small blob objects\",\n  \"dst\": \"canonical frame-and-dot diagram preserving containment and sibling row/column order\",\n  \"transform\": \"replace each blob by one pixel; replace each frame by a rectangular border padded around its direct children; keep outside blobs outside the enclosing frame\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"canonical crop; root-level outside blobs occupy a 3x3 bg slot\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b3084d4\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3217\nmechanism=0.3692\nlexical=0.0106\ncode_feature=0.5714\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:output_resize, code:rotation, object:grid_edge, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:crop_resize, operation:rotate, relation:between, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/7b3084d4.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/7b3084d4.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nAssemble colored rectangular sprites into a single tiled rectangle, anchored by a marker pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Several solid-colored rectangular sprites scattered on the grid.\n- One sprite has a unique single-pixel marker (different color) at one corner — this is the anchor.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the anchor sprite (contains the marker pixel) and the remaining pieces.\n- Rotate the anchor so the marker pixel lands at position (0,0) of the output.\n- Pack all remaining pieces (allowing 90° rotations) to tile a complete rectangle with no gaps.\n\nKey insight: The lone marker pixel picks the anchor and fixes the output orientation — it must sit at the top-left corner.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Assemble colored rectangular sprites like puzzle pieces using the grey-cornered orange block as the anchor, fitting other sprites by matching their edge positions.\",\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"colored rectangular sprites without grey corner marker s\",\n  \"dst\": \"anchor sprite (orange block with grey corner pixel) or previously attached sprites d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"edge(s) aligns_with open_edge(d) such that union forms contiguous_tiled_rectangle\",\n  \"path\": \"\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"\",\n  \"transform\": \"rotate(anchor, until grey_pixel at top_left)\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": null,\n  \"grid_shape\": \"tight crop\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"repeat merge until all sprites assembled into single rectangle\",\n  \"concepts\": [\n    \"jigsaw\",\n    \"assembly\",\n    \"anchor\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"The grey corner pixel identifies the anchor sprite and determines output orientation (grey stays at top-left corner). Each sprite is a solid colored rectangle; they tile together to form a complete rectangular output.\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ac6f9922\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3316\nmechanism=0.3961\nlexical=0.0113\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, object:closed_region, object:grid_edge, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:sort_order, relation:adjacent, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/ac6f9922.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/ac6f9922.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSummarize the enclosed table slots by the single object color inside each slot, using the slot background color for empty slots.\n\nInput structure\n- The most common color forms the outer background and all enclosed slot\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
